# Rank 1232 / CVE-2019-17638: Jetty's HTTP/1 response generator returns the
# same pooled response-header ByteBuffer to the buffer pool twice when a
# response header does not fit, so the pool hands one buffer to two
# connections at once and one client receives header bytes -- Set-Cookie,
# Authorization, session identifiers -- belonging to another client's
# response (CWE-415 double free, whose consequence here is CWE-200 exposure).
#
# HttpConnection.SendCallback.process() drives HttpGenerator in a loop. On
# HEADER_OVERFLOW the vulnerable revision reads _header.capacity(), calls
# _bufferPool.release(_header) and only then tests whether the buffer is
# at the configured response-header size; when it is, the method
# throws BadMessageException(500) with the _header field still pointing at
# the buffer it just released. The throw unwinds into the callback's failure
# path, which runs the callback's own cleanup: `if (_header != null)
# _bufferPool.release(_header); _header = null;`. The field is not null, so
# the same buffer is released a second time and sits in the pool twice.
# Reaching it needs nothing but a response whose headers exceed
# responseHeaderSize, which an application controls through any header it
# echoes back.
#
# The fixed revision moves the size test ahead of the release, extracts
# releaseHeader()/releaseChunk() helpers that null the field they release,
# and calls those helpers from both the overflow branch and the cleanup
# path, so no path can release a buffer the field still points at. The same
# commit moves _chunk from the connection to the callback, which removes a
# second copy of the same aliasing problem, and adds LargeHeaderTest.
#
# The pool row names eclipse/jetty.project. That organisation redirects to
# jetty/jetty.project: the project moved organisations and kept its history,
# so the row and the row that names jetty/jetty.project for CVE-2025-5115
# are the same repository under its old and current owner, not a mislink.
# Cloning has to follow the redirect, and ff8ae56fa9 is reachable there.
#
# ATTENTION, engine gap. Nothing is labelled on either revision: `vyql match`
# on a minimal pair reports no source, no sink and no control, and `vyql
# resolve` shows both `_bufferPool.release` call sites terminating
# unresolved, because no Java binding names a buffer-pool acquire or release
# at all. `vyql scan --cache off --max-ram 4GB` over the real jetty-server
# module (252 java files) reports 165 findings, none of them on
# HttpConnection.java.
#
# Filling the binding gap alone is measured and does not work. A probe
# binding emitting code.PointerFree at `callee.method == "release"` labels
# both call sites (`vyql match` confirms), and still reports nothing on
# either revision, because PotentialDoubleFree (VYQL-MEM-008) asks for one
# code.PointerFree to reach another and the two releases here sit in
# different methods joined only by an exception unwinding into a framework
# callback. The same probe does report VYQL-MEM-008 for two release calls
# written one after another inside a single method, which places the
# boundary exactly. It also costs precision immediately: on the real
# jetty-server module it produces one VYQL-MEM-008, on
# HttpOutput.java:1081/1091, where the buffer is reassigned (`out = bigger`)
# between the two releases and the code is correct; and the same rule fires
# on a two-line method releasing two distinct buffers, which is ordinary
# pooled-buffer code.
#
# The discriminating fact is the one the fixed revision adds: whether the field holding
# the released buffer is cleared before a path that can leave the method,
# given a sibling method that releases the same field under a null test.
# C and C++ have a frontend analysis for the neighbouring shape,
# `lifetime.release_then_return` (ccLifetimeReleaseReturnObservations in
# internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/c.go), which is what
# code.FailurePathOwnedBufferFree / VYQL-MEM-011 is built on; the Java
# frontend emits no lifetime observation of any kind, and the data layer
# cannot substitute for one, since the pattern kinds a binding can query are
# callExpr, binaryExpr, addExpr, isExpr and presenceNode -- none of which
# states a relation between a release statement, a field assignment that
# does not follow it, and an exit path. No definition change attempted, per
# this loop's instruction not to do engine work.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-engine-gap, both revisions rejected.

test "rank1232: the response header buffer is released with the field still set before a throw"
  lang java
  file jetty-server/src/main/java/org/eclipse/jetty/server/HttpConnection.java
  reject VYQL-MEM-007
  reject VYQL-MEM-008
  reject VYQL-MEM-011
  code
  ```
  package org.eclipse.jetty.server;

  import java.nio.ByteBuffer;

  import org.eclipse.jetty.http.BadMessageException;
  import org.eclipse.jetty.http.HttpGenerator;
  import org.eclipse.jetty.io.ByteBufferPool;

  public class HttpConnection
  {
      private ByteBufferPool _bufferPool;
      private HttpConfiguration _config;
      private HttpGenerator _generator;

      private class SendCallback
      {
          private ByteBuffer _header;

          public Action process() throws Exception
          {
              while (true)
              {
                  HttpGenerator.Result result = _generator.generateResponse(_header);
                  switch (result)
                  {
                      case NEED_HEADER:
                      {
                          _header = _bufferPool.acquire(_config.getOutputBufferSize(), true);
                          continue;
                      }
                      case HEADER_OVERFLOW:
                      {
                          int capacity = _header.capacity();
                          _bufferPool.release(_header);
                          if (capacity >= _config.getResponseHeaderSize())
                              throw new BadMessageException(500, "Response header too large");
                          _header = _bufferPool.acquire(_config.getResponseHeaderSize(), true);
                          continue;
                      }
                      default:
                          return Action.IDLE;
                  }
              }
          }

          protected boolean onCompleteFailure(Throwable failure)
          {
              if (_header != null)
                  _bufferPool.release(_header);
              _header = null;
              return true;
          }
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1232: the size test runs first and every release clears the field it releases"
  lang java
  file jetty-server/src/main/java/org/eclipse/jetty/server/HttpConnection.java
  reject VYQL-MEM-007
  reject VYQL-MEM-008
  reject VYQL-MEM-011
  code
  ```
  package org.eclipse.jetty.server;

  import java.nio.ByteBuffer;

  import org.eclipse.jetty.http.BadMessageException;
  import org.eclipse.jetty.http.HttpGenerator;
  import org.eclipse.jetty.io.ByteBufferPool;

  public class HttpConnection
  {
      private ByteBufferPool _bufferPool;
      private HttpConfiguration _config;
      private HttpGenerator _generator;

      private class SendCallback
      {
          private ByteBuffer _header;

          public Action process() throws Exception
          {
              while (true)
              {
                  HttpGenerator.Result result = _generator.generateResponse(_header);
                  switch (result)
                  {
                      case NEED_HEADER:
                      {
                          _header = _bufferPool.acquire(_config.getOutputBufferSize(), true);
                          continue;
                      }
                      case HEADER_OVERFLOW:
                      {
                          if (_header.capacity() >= _config.getResponseHeaderSize())
                              throw new BadMessageException(500, "Response header too large");
                          releaseHeader();
                          _header = _bufferPool.acquire(_config.getResponseHeaderSize(), true);
                          continue;
                      }
                      default:
                          return Action.IDLE;
                  }
              }
          }

          protected boolean onCompleteFailure(Throwable failure)
          {
              releaseHeader();
              return true;
          }

          private void releaseHeader()
          {
              if (_header != null)
                  _bufferPool.release(_header);
              _header = null;
          }
      }
  }
  ```
